00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.600
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.900
In square ABCD

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:12.080
construct a quarter circle centered at A whose radius is equal to the side length of the square.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:19.120
Construct a circle P inside the quarter circle that is tangent to both the quarter circle and the square

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.050
Finally construct CE

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:29.880
such that CE is tangent to the inscribed circle. The question is what is the measure of angle ECD?

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:36.809
Pause the video, if you'd like to give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.649
Recall that if two circles are tangent their centers and the tangent point are collinear

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:03.059
This is because AC is perpendicular to the tangent line and BC is also perpendicular to the tangent line

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.880
Therefore ACB forms a straight line segment.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.979
This is true for both externally and internally tangent circles.

00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:13.979
Now, let's solve the problem

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:20.009
We have a quarter circle centered at A and we have an inscribed circle P that's tangent to it

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:24.269
Therefore AP and the tangent point will be collinear

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:31.709
Since A is a corner of the square this line segment will also go through C the other corner of the square

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.669
Suppose the inscribed circle has a radius equal to r

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.279
Construct PQ such that Q is the tangent point alongside AD

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.129
PQ will have a length equal to r

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:52.709
Now construct the perpendicular radius, which will also have a length equal to r. This means AQ has length equal to r.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:02:01.319
Thus AP is the hypotenuse of an isosceles right triangle and its length will be equal to r times the square root of 2

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:07.979
We then have the radius of the quarter circle is equal to r square root of 2 plus r

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:15.509
This means AD will have the same length and thus QD must have a length equal to r times the square root of 2

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.559
Now let's consider the diagonal of the square

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:25.590
This must be equal to the square root of 2 multiplied by the side AD

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:30.239
We can substitute in for the length of AD and then simplify

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.969
we know the length of AC and

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.919
We know that a portion of it is equal to r plus r times the square root of 2

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.489
Therefore the remaining portion must be equal to r

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:44.488
From here. Let's construct CE

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:50.550
Since E is a tangent point we must have P. EC is a right angle

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.860
Then PE is equal to R because it's a radius of the circle

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.520
now consider the right triangle PCE

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.129
We have hypotenuse that's equal to 2r. And a shorter leg that's equal to r

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.879
Therefore the angle opposite r must be 30 degrees because this is a special right triangle

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.000
This means PCE is equal to 30 degrees

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.779
We can finally calculate the measure of angle ECD

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.099
It'll be equal to angle ACD minus angle PCE

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.580
Angle ACD is equal to 45 degrees and angle PCE is equal to 30 degrees .

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.260
Therefore, angle ECD is equal to 15 degrees and that's our answer

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.610
It's a cute little geometry problem. Things are making mind your decisions

